{"content":"Found the root cause. The traceback showed the crash originates in `sqlparse/filters/reindent.py`'s `_next_token`, at line 62: `if not (token and token.normalized == 'BETWEEN'):`. That extra `not` is an injected bug (mutation) - it inverts the intended condition. The correct/original logic recurses into `_next_token` only when the found token IS `BETWEEN` (to skip past the paired `AND`), not when it *isn't*. With the inverted condition, `_next_token` recurses even when no split-keyword token was found at all (`token is None`), passing `tidx=None` into the recursive call, which flows into `Token.token_next_by(idx=None)` and crashes at `idx += 1` in sqlparse/sql.py because `idx` is `None`.

This single inverted condition explains the broad blast radius: `_split_kwds`/`_next_token` is invoked by the reindent filter for essentially any SQL with no split keywords in a sub-list, so nearly all formatting/reindent paths (`sqlformat` CLI usage, "normal sql still works", DOS-prevention checks that reindent inputs, etc.) trip this TypeError - matching "'valid args'; 'cli multiple files with inplace'; 'do sprevention: normal sql still works'; and 38 more".

Fix applied: removed the erroneous `not`, restoring the original semantics:
```python
if token and token.normalized == 'BETWEEN':
    tidx, token = self._next_token(tlist, tidx)
    if token and token.normalized == 'AND':
        tidx, token = self._next_token(tlist, tidx)
return tidx, token
```

